<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Freud asked for a same-day visit because the roof of his mouth and the edges of the defect have burned for five days, worse with hot or spicy food. He has worn the new obturator day and night since it was fitted three weeks ago, because he finds speaking and swallowing easier with it in, and he has been rinsing it rather than brushing it. He has noticed white patches on the palate. He has no fever, bleeding, lump, new numbness or trouble swallowing, and no leakage into his nose. He has not smoked since the quit date and is taking varenicline 1 mg twice daily, the 14 mg patch and a few nicotine lozenges a day, and acetaminophen 1,000 mg three times daily. He asked whether this could be the cancer coming back. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Five days ago, three weeks after the new obturator was fitted</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Palate and the edges of the maxillectomy defect</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Five days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Burning soreness</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Pain 4 of 10</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Constant, worse with hot or spicy food</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Wearing the obturator day and night and rinsing rather than brushing it</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Better with cool drinks</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>White patches; no fever, bleeding, lump, numbness or trouble swallowing</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

