    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Candida stomatitis under the obturator (B37.0)</content>. Redness in the shape of the prosthesis with white plaques that wipe off is typical of Candida infection under a prosthesis worn day and night. There is no ulcer, mass or induration to suggest a recurrence, but any area that is not normal two weeks after treatment needs examination by his head and neck surgeon and a biopsy. [S]<list><item>Nystatin suspension 5 mL four times daily for 14 days with the obturator out.</item><item>Brush the obturator twice daily and soak it overnight in the cleanser recommended by Dr. Vasquez-Lindqvist; leave it out for at least six hours each night.</item><item>Recheck the mouth at the wellness visit in two weeks; head and neck surgeon and biopsy for any area that has not healed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tobacco use disorder, severe, in early remission (F17.290)</content>. No smoking for eight weeks. Stopping smoking will help the mouth heal. Nicotine lozenges can be continued, used away from the sore areas. [S]<list><item>Continue varenicline 1 mg twice daily and the 14 mg patch until the wellness visit, as planned.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic pain at the maxillectomy site (G89.29)</content>. Pain up from 3 to 4 with the infection. [S]<list><item>Continue acetaminophen 1,000 mg three times daily; no more than 3,000 mg a day.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Initiating Pharmacologic Treatment in Tobacco-Dependent Adults. An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2020); NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Head and Neck Cancers, Version 2.2026 (follow-up and survivorship) (2026); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Candidiasis: 2016 Update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Wellness visit in two weeks with a mouth check. Return sooner for fever, bleeding, a lump, or trouble swallowing. Next scheduled visit 1930-09-28: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
